\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Prove that natural i.i.d. first-passage-percolation models on $\mathbb{Z}^d$, including exponential edge lengths, almost surely contain no two-sided infinite geodesic.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~itai/erd100.pdf#page=9}{[S2]}, \href{https://arquivo.pt/noFrame/replay/20201231041538id_/http://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~itai/erd100.pdf}{[S3]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Prove that natural i.i.d. first-passage-percolation models on $\mathbb{Z}^d$, including exponential edge lengths, almost surely contain no two-sided infinite geodesic.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 10000073 (AMR-099-0073). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~itai/erd100.pdf#page=9}.
\item[S3] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://arquivo.pt/noFrame/replay/20201231041538id_/http://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~itai/erd100.pdf}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:10000073}

